.TH TRASH 1 "2026-11-11" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash \- move files to the platform trash instead of deleting them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.I FILE ...
.br
.B trash
.B \-\-empty\-trash
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash
is an
.BR rm (1)\-compatible
front end that moves each operand to the platform trash instead of unlinking
it.  A directory still requires
.BR \-r ,
.B \-d
accepts only empty directories, missing operands fail unless
.B \-f
is given, and
.B .
and
.B ..
are always refused.  The data survives in a trash directory and can usually
be restored from the file manager.
.PP
It is deployed as
.I ~/bin/trash
by
.BR dots\-setup (1)
from
.IR common/bin/bin/trash .
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B macOS
.I ~/.Trash
for the home volume, and
.I /.Trashes/<uid>
on other volumes.  Entries are stored directly in the trash directory.
Finder shows them; the metadata needed for \(lqPut Back\(rq is written only
when
.I TRASH_MACOS_FINDER=1
is set and a GUI session is available.
.TP
.B Linux
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
(default
.IR ~/.local/share/Trash )
for the home filesystem, and
.I /.Trash/<uid>
or
.I /.Trash\-<uid>
on other filesystems.  The freedesktop.org layout is used:
.I files/
holds the entries and
.I info/*.trashinfo
records each original path and deletion time, so file managers can restore
them.
.TP
.B BSD
.I ~/.trash
directly; BSD has no system trash facility.
.PP
The
.B home
backend always uses
.I $TRASH_DIR
when set, otherwise
.IR ~/.trash ,
on every platform.
.B auto
selects the system facility on Linux and macOS and the home backend
elsewhere;
.B system
does the same, falling back to the home backend where no system facility
exists.
.PP
A target on another filesystem uses that volume's trash when possible, so
the move remains a rename.  If no per-volume trash is usable, the tree is
copied and the original removed.  Name collisions are resolved with
.IR .1 ,
.IR .2 ,
and so on, keeping the
.I .trashinfo
name in step.
.SH SAFETY
These checks run before anything moves, even with
.BR \-f :
.TP
.B . and ..
Refused as the final path component, as with
.BR rm (1).
.TP
.B /
Refused, and not overridable with
.BR \-\-no\-preserve\-root :
every trash directory is inside
.IR / ,
so moving (or copying and deleting) the root filesystem is never attempted.
The flag is accepted for command-line compatibility only.
.TP
.B A path containing the trash directory
Refused.  This catches
.IR $HOME ,
an XDG data parent such as
.IR ~/.local ,
or a mount point whose per-volume trash lives inside it, so
.B trash \-r ~
fails instead of recursing.
.TP
.B A mount point
Refused.
.BR rm (1)
reports the same case as
.IR "Device or resource busy" .
Renaming a mount point is impossible, and the copy fallback could duplicate
an entire filesystem.
.SH PROMPTS
.B \-i
prompts once per operand.  A directory moves atomically, so there is no
per-file descend prompt.  A declined prompt is not an error; the command
exits 0, matching
.BR "rm \-i" .
A path that is not writable also gets a one-off prompt when standard input
is a terminal and neither
.B \-f
nor an explicit
.B \-\-interactive
option was given.
.PP
.B \-I
asks once before moving more than three operands, or before a recursive
directory operand.
.PP
.B \-f
and
.B \-i
are applied left to right, so the last one controls prompting
.RB ( \-fi
prompts,
.B \-if
does not).  As with GNU
.BR rm ,
.B \-f
keeps its other effect regardless of order: nonexistent operands stay
silent.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent operands and never prompt.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Prompt before every move.
.TP
.B \-I
Prompt once before more than three operands, or a recursive directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-interactive= WHEN
.I WHEN
is
.BR never ,
.BR once ,
or
.BR always .
.B never
also skips the
.B \-\-empty\-trash
confirmation.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Move directories and their contents.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir
Move empty directories; a non-empty directory is still an error.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print
.I trashed 'FILE' \-> 'DEST'
for each move, and a count for each emptied trash.
.TP
.BI \-\-backend= WHEN
.I WHEN
is
.BR auto
(default),
.BR system ,
or
.BR home .
.TP
.B \-\-empty\-trash
Empty the trash; list the locations and ask for confirmation.
.TP
.B \-\-all
With
.BR \-\-empty\-trash ,
also empty per-volume trashes.
.TP
.B \-\-preserve\-root
Refuse
.I /
(the default).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-preserve\-root
Accepted for
.BR rm (1)
compatibility;
.I /
is still refused.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Unknown options, including
.B rm
flags that have no meaning for a move
.RB ( \-x ,
.BR \-\-one\-file\-system ),
are rejected rather than silently ignored.
.SH EMPTYING THE TRASH
.B trash \-\-empty\-trash
prints every location it is about to clear, then asks
.I Are you sure? [y/N]
on standard error and reads the answer from standard input.  With the
default
.B auto
backend it clears the system trash and
.I ~/.trash
when that directory has anything in it (either may have been used by this
tool).
.B \-\-backend=system
limits it to the system trash and
.B \-\-backend=home
to the home trash.
.PP
.B \-\-all
adds per-volume trashes:
.I /Volumes/*/.Trashes/<uid>
on macOS and, on Linux, the
.IR .Trash\-<uid> ,
.I .Trash/<uid>
and
.I .Trashes/<uid>
directories of filesystems listed in
.I /proc/mounts .
Pseudo-filesystems and network or generic FUSE mounts (NFS, CIFS/SMB, SSHFS,
AFS, 9P) are skipped, only existing directories are considered, and other
users' trashes are never touched.
.PP
The confirmation needs a terminal.  Without one the command refuses and
exits 1 unless
.B \-f
(or
.BR \-\-interactive=never )
was given.  Declining deletes nothing and exits 1; an already-empty trash is
reported and exits 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRASH_DIR
Location used by the
.B home
backend (default
.IR ~/.trash ).
A leading
.I ~/
is expanded.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Parent of the Linux home trash
.RB ( "$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash" ,
default
.IR ~/.local/share ).
.TP
.B TRASH_MACOS_FINDER
Set to
.B 1
to move files through Finder, which records the metadata needed for Put
Back.  Works only in a GUI session and falls back to a direct move when
Finder cannot be used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash/files/
Linux home trash entries.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash/info/
The
.I .trashinfo
metadata that records each original path and deletion time.
.TP
.I <mount>/.Trash/<uid>
.I <mount>/.Trash\-<uid>
Linux per-volume trashes.
.TP
.I ~/.Trash
.I <mount>/.Trashes/<uid>
macOS trashes.
.TP
.I $TRASH_DIR
.I ~/.trash
The home backend.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand moved, or deliberately skipped by
.B \-i
or
.BR \-I ;
or the trash was already empty.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, at least one operand could not be moved, or
.B \-\-empty\-trash
was declined or interrupted, or a deletion failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Move one file and one directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&trash notes.txt
\&trash \-r build/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prompt before each of a glob, like
.BR "rm \-i" :
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&trash \-i *.tmp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Move an empty directory without
.BR \-r :
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&trash \-d empty\-dir
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show where everything went:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&trash \-v old\-*.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a single portable trash directory on every platform:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&TRASH_DIR=~/trash\-bin trash \-\-backend=home report.pdf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Empty the home and per-volume trashes without the question:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&trash \-\-empty\-trash \-\-all \-f
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR dots\-setup (1)
